NAME
       dmesg - print the kernel's message buffer

SYNOPSIS
       dmesg

DESCRIPTION
       The kernel keeps its recent messages in a ring buffer in memory:
       what hardware it found at boot, disks being added, file systems being
       mounted, drivers reporting errors. dmesg prints that buffer, each line
       starting with the seconds since boot. On RHEL only root may read it.

       Look here when a new disk does not show up or a device misbehaves.
       journalctl -k shows the same kernel messages with real times, and also
       for earlier boots when the journal is persistent.

EXAMPLES
       Did the kernel see the second disk?

           dmesg | grep vdb

SEE ALSO
       journalctl(1), lsblk(8)
